{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791192502,"nonce":"rKE3Hvnij0q090hQEyitCQYp/gw=","title":"Module-private class does not shadow the StdTypes type of the same name in `new`","message":"In unit/issues/Issue6833.hx the module declares `private class KeyValueIterator\u003cK,V\u003e` and calls `new KeyValueIterator([1 =\u003e 'hello'])`. The TAST binds that `new` to a builtin std symbol (SymbolId(9), kind Class, name \"Iterator\", no qualified name; class type TypeId(34)) instead of the module's own `unit.issues.KeyValueIterator` (SymbolId(11) in the HIR). No constructor resolves by type, so a 1-arg `new` value-wraps: the expression evaluates to the Map argument and the real constructor never runs. The row passes only because `noAssert()` does.\n\nMIR now treats such a class as local by bare name (`declares_class` in mir/helpers/classes.rs) so it keeps the value-wrap instead of running the real ctor, which crashes (see the Map\u003cK,V\u003e field issue).\n\nFix belongs in TAST name resolution: a module-local type shadows a std-level type of the same name, as in Haxe.\n\nRepro: RAYZOR_CTOR_DEBUG=1 on Issue6833 shows `[CTOR] class=KeyValueIterator path=none class_type=TypeId(34) fid=None`.","files":null}]}